        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Chest freezers are simple machines, and that is their strength. The lid opens upward so the cold air stays put, the insulation is thick, and the compressor is small. That makes them cheap to run, slow to warm up when the power goes out, and easy to keep going on a portable power station or generator. The decisions that matter are size, where it will live, and whether that spot gets hot or cold.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Actually Matters in a Chest Freezer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most chest freezers in the 3.5 to 7 cubic foot range are built the same way: a steel cabinet with foam insulation, a compressor at the back, a mechanical thermostat dial, cooling coils wrapped inside the walls, and a hanging wire basket. That sameness is good news for buyers. You do not need a premium brand to get a reliable freezer, and the differences that matter are few and easy to check.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first is capacity, which we cover in the next section. The second is where the freezer will live. A standard freezer is designed for a conditioned room, and manufacturers list an ambient temperature range it is rated to run in. If it is going in an unheated garage, a shed, a porch or a hot utility room, you want a model sold as garage-ready, with a wider rated range. That one detail matters more than anything else on the spec sheet for garage owners.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third is energy use, which you can read straight off the yellow EnergyGuide label as kilowatt-hours per year. Small chest freezers are among the most efficient appliances in a house, and the spread between models of the same size is usually modest. Divide the annual figure by 365 and you get the daily number that matters for backup power, which is why we keep coming back to it in this guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            After that come conveniences: a defrost drain near the floor, a basket or two, a lid that stays open on its own, a power-on light, and on some models an external temperature control instead of a dial at the back. Nice to have, but none of them should outweigh capacity, placement and efficiency.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chest or upright? Uprights are easier to organize and often have auto-defrost, but they lose more cold air every time the door opens, and auto-defrost costs energy and can dry out food. For outage resilience, efficiency and price, chest freezers win. That is why we focus on them here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Cubic Feet per Household: Sizing a Chest Freezer
          </h2>

          <p>
            A common rule of thumb is about 1.5 cubic feet of freezer space per person in the household, on top of the freezer compartment in your refrigerator. Each cubic foot holds roughly 30 to 35 pounds of food when packed reasonably well. By that math, a couple is well served by a small chest freezer of 3.5 to 5 cubic feet, and a family of four by 7 cubic feet, more if you buy meat in bulk, hunt, or garden.
          </p>

          <p>
            Small chest freezers, the 3.5 cubic foot class, are the apartment-size freezer most people are actually looking for. They are roughly the footprint of a large cooler, fit in a closet, a pantry corner or under a counter-height surface, and can be carried by two people up a flight of stairs. They make the most sense for one or two people who want room for batch cooking and sale buys.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 5 cubic foot class is the sweet spot for most households. It holds a meaningful stockpile, still fits in a laundry room or garage corner, and draws little power. The 7 cubic foot class is the largest size that still feels like a small appliance, and for a family it is often worth stepping up to rather than buying a second freezer later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not oversize by too much. An empty freezer is less efficient per pound of food, and a deep chest that is two-thirds empty becomes a pit where food gets lost and freezer-burned. A full freezer also holds its temperature far longer in an outage, which we cover below. If you are between sizes and do not have a specific reason to stock more, buy the smaller one and keep it full.
          </p>

          <p>
            Measure before you buy, including the lid swing. A chest freezer needs clearance above it to open the lid fully, plus a few inches around the sides and back for airflow to the coils. Check doorways too. A 7 cubic foot freezer is usually manageable, but cabinets have different widths and depths, so measure the actual model's listed dimensions against your space.
          </p>
