        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a standard freezer in an unheated garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Freezers are rated for a range of surrounding temperatures. A standard model in a garage that drops near freezing or bakes in summer can struggle, run constantly or stop working properly, and operating outside the range may void the warranty. Buy a garage-ready model and check its rated range against your garage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Defeating the garage GFCI</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Code requires GFCI protection for garage receptacles, including the freezer's. Swapping in a standard receptacle to avoid nuisance trips removes real shock protection. If the GFCI trips repeatedly, have a licensed electrician find out why, and use a freezer alarm so any trip is caught before food thaws.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying far more freezer than you will fill</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An oversized, half-empty chest wastes energy, loses food at the bottom and warms faster in an outage because air holds little cold. Size at roughly 1.5 cubic feet per person, buy the smaller size when in doubt, and fill any empty space with jugs of water.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a generator in or near the garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Generators produce carbon monoxide that can kill within minutes. Run one outdoors only, well away from doors, windows and vents, never in a garage even with the door open, and keep working CO alarms inside. Feed the freezer with a rated outdoor extension cord or a professionally installed transfer switch.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Chipping ice off the walls</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The cooling coils in a chest freezer run inside the walls, and a knife, screwdriver or ice pick can puncture one and end the freezer. Unplug it, let the ice soften with the lid open and bowls of hot water inside, then use a plastic scraper and towels.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

