          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size chest freezer do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A common rule of thumb is about 1.5 cubic feet per person, on top of your refrigerator's freezer. One or two people are usually well served by 3.5 to 5 cubic feet, and a family of four by about 7 cubic feet, more if you buy meat in bulk, hunt or garden. Buy the smaller size if unsure, and keep it full.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put a chest freezer in my garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if it is rated for the temperatures your garage reaches. Standard freezers are designed for conditioned rooms and can struggle in very cold or very hot garages. Garage-ready models are rated for a wider ambient range, which is listed in the specs. Plug it into the GFCI-protected receptacle code requires, and add a temperature alarm.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a chest freezer stay frozen without power?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The USDA says a full freezer holds a safe temperature for about 48 hours with the door closed, and a half-full one for about 24 hours. Chest freezers tend to do well because cold air stays put when the lid opens. Keep it full, fill gaps with water jugs, and keep the lid shut during an outage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable power station run a chest freezer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A small chest freezer draws modest running watts and cycles on and off, with a brief starting surge. A quality power station with a pure sine wave inverter and around 500 watts or more of continuous output usually handles it. Capacity sets runtime: a station of about 1 kilowatt-hour can typically carry a small freezer for roughly a day.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much electricity does a chest freezer use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Small chest freezers in the 3.5 to 7 cubic foot range commonly use a few hundred kilowatt-hours a year according to their EnergyGuide labels, which is well under a kilowatt-hour a day and a few dollars a month at typical rates. A hot garage, frequent opening and heavy frost push it higher. A plug-in energy monitor shows your actual number.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I defrost a chest freezer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Defrost when the frost reaches about a quarter inch thick, which for most households is once or twice a year. Move the food into coolers, unplug the freezer, open the lid and let the ice soften, using the drain plug if your model has one. Never chip ice with sharp tools, since the cooling coils run inside the walls.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a chest freezer better than an upright for power outages?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally yes. Cold air sinks, so lifting a chest lid loses less of it than opening an upright door, and chest freezers usually have thick insulation and manual defrost, which keeps temperatures steady. That combination means a chest freezer tends to stay frozen longer without power and uses less energy when you are running it on a power station or generator.
              </p>
            </div>

